Injection points for the port's tests, under the names of three of Apache
Cloudberry's faults inside PostgreSQL's own commit and checkpoint, which
checkpoint_dtx_info in its isolation2 schedule sets: a commit held with
its record written and pg_xact not yet updated, so that a checkpoint
waits for it; the checkpoint come to that wait; and the checkpoint's
reckoning of the WAL its replication slots keep, where the test fails the
node, once the checkpoint is written:

  start_insertedDistributedCommitted    xact.c, RecordTransactionCommit()
  before_wait_VirtualXIDsDelayingChkpt  xlog.c, CreateCheckPoint()
  keep_log_seg                          xlog.c, KeepLogSeg()

Cloudberry's first is in its distributed commit alone, after the record it
writes for one (insertedDistributedCommitted(), cdbtm.c); the port's
distributed commit is the coordinator's own, an ordinary commit record
(gp_dtx.c), so the point is after that record in the critical section that
makes a checkpoint wait, run from the cache it is loaded into before it,
and gp_core fires the fault there only for a transaction whose parts it
prepared on the segments (gp_fault.c).  keep_log_seg's point gives its
callback a bool to set where a "skip" asks, which has the slots keep
nothing, as Cloudberry's skip does.  xact.c is given utils/injection_point.h
after its other headers: vanilla PostgreSQL 19's has not got it, where the
core series' O29 includes it among them, and it guards itself against a
second include.

gp_core's fault injector attaches a fault of Cloudberry's name to the point
(gp_fault.c).  They serve tests alone, so they are not in the core series:
Dockerfile.pg applies this after test-injection-points.patch, to the tree
of either ref, vanilla or patched, so that the two images still differ
only in the ref.  Dormant, as any point is: without injection points the
macro is empty, and with them a point nothing attached to runs no
callback.


